WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.920 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.860 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:18.630 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.660 align:middle line:84%
CASEY RODRIGUEZ: OK, let's
continue our discussion

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:22.620 align:middle line:90%
of measurable sets.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.160 align:middle line:90%


00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:30.500 align:middle line:84%
Let me just briefly recall
for you, the end of last time,

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.850 align:middle line:84%
we discussed some
general notions,

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:40.350 align:middle line:84%
some special collections
of subsets of R,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.240 align:middle line:84%
one of those being
an algebra, which

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.490 align:middle line:84%
is closed under taking
complements and taking

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:45.750 align:middle line:90%
finite unions.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:49.050 align:middle line:84%
And then we said a collection
of subsets of R's is a sigma

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:55.290 align:middle line:84%
algebra if it's also closed
under taking countable unions.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:59.040 align:middle line:84%
And not every algebra
is a sigma algebra.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.720 align:middle line:90%
Why did we bring all this up?

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.740 align:middle line:84%
Well, we were in the middle of
discussing Lebesgue measurable

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:06.240 align:middle line:90%
sets.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:17.830 align:middle line:84%
So recall that we say E
is Lebesgue measurable

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:26.860 align:middle line:84%
if for all subsets of R
A, the outer measure of A

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:32.050 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the outer
measure of A intersect E

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:37.240 align:middle line:84%
plus the outer measure of
A intersect the complement.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.690 align:middle line:90%


00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.970 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense,
a measurable set

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:48.130 align:middle line:84%
is one that divides sets nicely
with respect to outer measure.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.520 align:middle line:90%


00:01:51.520 --> 00:02:02.230 align:middle line:84%
And we denoted script M to
be the set of all E such

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:07.930 align:middle line:90%
that E is Lebesgue measurable.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.300 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to stop
saying Lebesgue measurable

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.175 align:middle line:84%
and just say
measurable from now on.

00:02:12.175 --> 00:02:15.380 align:middle line:90%


00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.320 align:middle line:84%
And what we showed
last time, we showed

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.580 align:middle line:84%
that M, the set of
measurable subsets of R,

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.060 align:middle line:90%
do form an algebra.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.930 align:middle line:84%
So it follows from
the definition

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:30.290 align:middle line:84%
that if E is measurable, then
E complement is measurable.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:32.510 align:middle line:84%
But we also showed
that if I take

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:36.640 align:middle line:84%
a finite union of
measurable sets,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.050 align:middle line:84%
or a finite collection
of measurable sets,

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:42.092 align:middle line:90%
their union is measurable.

00:02:42.092 --> 00:02:43.550 align:middle line:84%
And what we're
going to do today is

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:47.600 align:middle line:84%
we're going to show that
the set, the collection

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.120 align:middle line:84%
of measurable, Lebesgue
measurable, subsets of R

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.510 align:middle line:90%
form a sigma algebra.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.730 align:middle line:84%
So they have this
stronger property.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:03.955 align:middle line:90%
And also that-- last time--

00:03:03.955 --> 00:03:07.500 align:middle line:90%


00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:08.910 align:middle line:84%
there's no way to
write this in--

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.920 align:middle line:90%


00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:17.370 align:middle line:84%
maybe not definition,
but notation--

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:29.120 align:middle line:84%
that B is the Borel sigma
algebra, which is, recall,

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.780 align:middle line:84%
we proved it at the
end of last time.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:34.640 align:middle line:84%
So we gave this as an
example of a sigma algebra.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:43.230 align:middle line:84%
This is the smallest
sigma algebra

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:50.170 align:middle line:90%
containing all open sets.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:54.420 align:middle line:84%
So any other sigma algebra
that contains all open sets

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.380 align:middle line:84%
contains the sigma algebra
B, the Borel sigma algebra.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:07.130 align:middle line:90%


00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:11.240 align:middle line:84%
So like I said, our
goal for this lecture

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:15.140 align:middle line:84%
is we're going to show that
this collection of Lebesgue

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.390 align:middle line:84%
measurable sets is
a sigma algebra.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:19.950 align:middle line:84%
We're going to show it contains
the Borel sigma algebra.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:24.350 align:middle line:90%
And then what is--

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:30.110 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I can even state
it now, or at least

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:31.610 align:middle line:90%
I'll just say in words.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:32.930 align:middle line:90%
What is Lebesgue measure?

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:36.350 align:middle line:84%
It's simply-- Lebesgue
measure of a measurable set

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:42.925 align:middle line:84%
will be the outer measure
of that set as long as it's

00:04:42.925 --> 00:04:44.890 align:middle line:90%
Lebesgue measurable.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.260 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:50.155 align:middle line:84%
going to show that M
is a sigma algebra.

00:04:50.155 --> 00:04:53.000 align:middle line:90%


00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:09.120 align:middle line:84%
Now, one preliminary
remark that or lemma

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.940 align:middle line:84%
that we're going to prove
is that so this condition 3

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.320 align:middle line:84%
says that you need to prove that
every countable collection is

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.580 align:middle line:84%
closed under taking unions,
to ensure that an algebra is

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.760 align:middle line:90%
a sigma algebra.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.460 align:middle line:84%
But in fact, you
don't have to check it

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.568 align:middle line:90%
for an arbitrary collection.

00:05:26.568 --> 00:05:28.110 align:middle line:84%
You really just need
to check it only

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.890 align:middle line:84%
for a countable collection
of disjoint subsets.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:38.330 align:middle line:84%
So that's the lemma
that we'll use

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:42.050 align:middle line:84%
when we prove that the
collection of Lebesgue

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:46.520 align:middle line:84%
measurable sets is a sigma
algebra is the following--

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:47.450 align:middle line:90%
so this is general.

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:49.340 align:middle line:84%
This has nothing
to do, necessarily,

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.200 align:middle line:90%
with Lebesgue measure.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:06:04.010 align:middle line:90%
Let A be an algebra in--

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:05.950 align:middle line:84%
so again, N here is
a natural number--

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:10.720 align:middle line:90%


00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:18.460 align:middle line:84%
a collection of elements
of A. So each of these

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:30.150 align:middle line:84%
is a subset of R. Then there
exists a countable collection F

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:54.250 align:middle line:84%
n of elements of A
that are disjoint--

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:57.310 align:middle line:90%


00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:02.700 align:middle line:84%
so if I take F n and F m,
and n does not equal m,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.890 align:middle line:84%
then their
intersection is empty--

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:17.190 align:middle line:84%
such that the union of the E n's
equals the union of the F n's.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:18.400 align:middle line:90%
So what does this say?

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:19.610 align:middle line:90%
This says if we have--

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:23.190 align:middle line:90%


00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:27.250 align:middle line:90%
let me make this a remark, then.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:29.695 align:middle line:90%
What is the conclusion of this?

00:07:29.695 --> 00:07:37.970 align:middle line:90%


00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:41.690 align:middle line:84%
We only need to check
this third condition

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:46.130 align:middle line:84%
for being a sigma algebra,
that a countable collection is

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:52.520 align:middle line:84%
closed under taking unions,
condition 3 for sigma algebra,

00:07:52.520 --> 00:08:08.710 align:middle line:84%
for disjoint
collections of elements.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:12.163 align:middle line:90%


00:08:12.163 --> 00:08:13.830 align:middle line:84%
This follows immediately
from this lemma

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:18.120 align:middle line:84%
because if I have any arbitrary
collection of elements

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:23.400 align:middle line:84%
of the algebra, then their union
is equal to a union of elements

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:28.800 align:middle line:84%
of A, where the union is
now over elements that

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.320 align:middle line:90%
are disjoint from each other.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.360 align:middle line:90%
So this is the point.

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:37.419 align:middle line:84%
I only need to check the
condition for an algebra

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:40.120 align:middle line:84%
to be a sigma
algebra by checking

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:43.720 align:middle line:84%
that countable unions
of disjoint sets

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:44.800 align:middle line:90%
remain in the algebra.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:51.800 align:middle line:90%


00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.225 align:middle line:90%
So the proof is not very hard.

00:08:53.225 --> 00:08:56.980 align:middle line:90%


00:08:56.980 --> 00:09:05.160 align:middle line:84%
So let G-- let's
call it something--

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:12.990 align:middle line:84%
G n to be the union k
equals 1 to n of E k.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:18.470 align:middle line:90%


00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:27.080 align:middle line:84%
So then, these are growing
because G n contains

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:33.680 align:middle line:84%
the first n E k's, and then n
plus 1, I tack on another one.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.870 align:middle line:84%
So G1 is contained in
G2 is contained in G3.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:47.180 align:middle line:84%
And this is just kind
of easy to check.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:50.180 align:middle line:84%
And I'll leave it to you that
the union of the E n's is equal

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:53.820 align:middle line:90%
to the union of the G n's.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:56.270 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the G n's are
really just unions of the E

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:58.460 align:middle line:90%
k's, finite unions of the E k's.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:03.480 align:middle line:90%
So this is pretty clear.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:08.040 align:middle line:90%


00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:11.190 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if you actually want to
sit down and do the argument,

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:17.640 align:middle line:84%
every element in G n is
contained in E1 up to E n.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:20.520 align:middle line:84%
And so every G n is
contained in this union.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:22.260 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
union of the G n's is

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:23.610 align:middle line:90%
contained in this union.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:25.950 align:middle line:84%
And then it's easy
to go the other way

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.498 align:middle line:84%
around showing this union
is contained in this union

00:10:28.498 --> 00:10:29.415 align:middle line:90%
so that they're equal.

00:10:29.415 --> 00:10:32.780 align:middle line:90%


00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:47.280 align:middle line:84%
And now, I take
F1 to be G1 and F

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:51.540 align:middle line:84%
n plus 1 to be F
n plus 1 take away

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:56.430 align:middle line:84%
F n for n bigger than or
equal to 1, or I should say--

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:06.730 align:middle line:84%
sorry about that, that should
be G n plus 1 take away G n.

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:09.130 align:middle line:90%
So what is this?

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:12.130 align:middle line:84%
I take the first set
to be G1 and then

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:15.520 align:middle line:84%
F2 will be what's
in G2 but not in G1.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:22.795 align:middle line:90%
So then, what is this?

00:11:22.795 --> 00:11:25.870 align:middle line:90%


00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:26.780 align:middle line:90%
What do we get?

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:29.980 align:middle line:84%
We get that the union
of any finite guy

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:33.070 align:middle line:90%
is equal to, in fact--

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:40.330 align:middle line:90%


00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:45.820 align:middle line:84%
so this is pretty clear, again,
because what am I doing here?

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:49.180 align:middle line:84%
F2 will be what's
in G2 take away G1.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:53.560 align:middle line:84%
What's in F3 is whatever's
in G3 take away G2.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:58.360 align:middle line:84%
And their union is going
to be the same as over here

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:02.230 align:middle line:84%
because I'm including
G1 and so on.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:03.550 align:middle line:90%
I hope this is clear.

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:07.980 align:middle line:90%


00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:10.170 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, I don't
know why I'm writing n.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:14.740 align:middle line:90%


00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:18.940 align:middle line:84%
We in fact get this union
is equal to this union.

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:21.290 align:middle line:84%
And again, I'm leaving
some details out,

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:25.000 align:middle line:84%
but you can check that
this union is contained

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:26.530 align:middle line:84%
in this union and
then this union

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:29.110 align:middle line:84%
is contained in this
union very easily.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:37.880 align:middle line:84%
But I hope the idea is pretty
clear because at each stage,

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:41.090 align:middle line:84%
you're taking whatever
is in this set,

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:45.920 align:middle line:84%
cutting out what was
appeared before it,

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:47.000 align:middle line:90%
in the sets before it.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:13:02.010 align:middle line:90%


00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:07.050 align:middle line:84%
So now, let's go back
to measurable sets.

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:11.700 align:middle line:90%
We're now going to show that--

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:17.180 align:middle line:84%
almost-- we are going to prove
that the collection of Lebesgue

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:20.280 align:middle line:84%
measurable sets is
a sigma algebra.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:25.000 align:middle line:84%
But first, we need
the following theorem,

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:26.070 align:middle line:90%
which is the following--

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:28.910 align:middle line:90%


00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:34.270 align:middle line:84%
Let A be a subset of R. And this
is essentially the whole game,

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:35.560 align:middle line:90%
as we'll see.

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:43.730 align:middle line:84%
Let A be a subset of R
E1 up to E n Lebesgue

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:49.060 align:middle line:84%
measurable, and not
just measurable,

00:13:49.060 --> 00:14:03.160 align:middle line:90%
but be disjoint measurable sets.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:04.930 align:middle line:84%
So I only have
finitely many of them.

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:08.610 align:middle line:90%


00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:17.230 align:middle line:84%
Then, the outer
measure of A intersect

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:28.730 align:middle line:84%
the union of these guys is
equal to the sum k equals 1 to n

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:38.540 align:middle line:84%
of the outer measure
of A intersect E k.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:54.280 align:middle line:90%


00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:59.790 align:middle line:84%
So this shouldn't come
as too much of a surprise

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:06.700 align:middle line:84%
because this is kind
of close to saying--

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:15.110 align:middle line:84%
I should say this is
very true if say E1--

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:16.820 align:middle line:90%
say you just have two sets--

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:20.210 align:middle line:90%
E1 is E and E2 is E complement.

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:22.490 align:middle line:84%
Then that's just the
definition of being measurable.

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:27.730 align:middle line:90%


00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:30.970 align:middle line:84%
And you build up from
that by using induction.

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:33.370 align:middle line:90%
That's the basic argument.

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:35.860 align:middle line:84%
Again, I'm sorry I keep moving
away from the chalkboard

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:39.160 align:middle line:84%
because I'm used to
lecturing to a classroom,

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:43.600 align:middle line:84%
even though it's been months now
since I've been in a classroom

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:45.670 align:middle line:90%
with students.

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:47.155 align:middle line:84%
And because I'm
a freak of nature

00:15:47.155 --> 00:15:49.030 align:middle line:84%
who writes from my left
hand, I have to write

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:51.100 align:middle line:90%
and then do this kind of move.

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:54.760 align:middle line:84%
But anyways, we're going
to prove this by induction.

00:15:54.760 --> 00:16:02.500 align:middle line:90%


00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:04.530 align:middle line:90%
So the proof is by induction.

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:09.040 align:middle line:90%


00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:11.220 align:middle line:90%
So n equals 1.

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:12.510 align:middle line:90%
That's just one set.

00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:13.275 align:middle line:90%
This is clear.

00:16:13.275 --> 00:16:16.330 align:middle line:90%


00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:19.750 align:middle line:84%
That's just the outer
measure of A intersect E1

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:22.720 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the outer
measure of A intersect E1.

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:23.575 align:middle line:90%
So that's fine.

00:16:23.575 --> 00:16:27.300 align:middle line:90%


00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:29.940 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
put a check there

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:31.680 align:middle line:90%
without writing anything down.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:38.680 align:middle line:84%
So now, let's label
this statement by star.

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:40.545 align:middle line:84%
So now, let's do
the induction step.

00:16:40.545 --> 00:16:47.990 align:middle line:90%


00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:58.490 align:middle line:84%
So suppose star
holds for n equals m.

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:03.320 align:middle line:84%
And what we now want to show,
meaning that if I have E1 up

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:09.619 align:middle line:84%
to up to E m disjoint measurable
sets, then star holds.

00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:12.485 align:middle line:84%
Now, I want to show that
statement holds for m plus 1.

00:17:12.485 --> 00:17:28.500 align:middle line:90%


00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:47.510 align:middle line:84%
So let E1 up to E m plus 1
be measurable disjoint sets.

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:48.827 align:middle line:90%
So each of them--

00:17:48.827 --> 00:17:49.910 align:middle line:90%
they're pairwise disjoint.

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:58.260 align:middle line:90%


00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:15.260 align:middle line:84%
Then, since E m plus 1 is
measurable, what do we have?

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:18.910 align:middle line:90%


00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:25.440 align:middle line:84%
Well, first, before I use that,
let me just note something.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:36.600 align:middle line:84%
Since E m plus 1 is just
disjoint from E1 up to E m,

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:40.080 align:middle line:84%
let A be a subset of R. We
want to verify star for A,

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:45.140 align:middle line:90%
so I need to have an A.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:51.800 align:middle line:84%
So since E k
intersect E m plus 1

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:58.150 align:middle line:84%
equals the empty set
for all k equals 1 to m,

00:18:58.150 --> 00:19:08.065 align:middle line:84%
we get that A intersect the
union k equals 1 m plus 1.

00:19:08.065 --> 00:19:11.000 align:middle line:90%


00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:12.320 align:middle line:90%
What does this equal to?

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:15.640 align:middle line:90%


00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:19.840 align:middle line:84%
Or then I intersect
that with E m plus 1,

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:25.780 align:middle line:84%
this is simply equal to
A intersect E m plus 1

00:19:25.780 --> 00:19:30.560 align:middle line:84%
because E1 up to E m--
these are disjoint.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:37.240 align:middle line:84%
So when I transfer
this through, I

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:40.930 align:middle line:84%
can write this intersection
by bringing this intersect,

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:44.590 align:middle line:84%
each of these E k's, where
k equals 1 to m plus 1.

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:47.530 align:middle line:84%
And then this is empty when
k is not equal to m plus 1.

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:53.360 align:middle line:84%
So I just pick up E m plus
1 and I get that there.

00:19:53.360 --> 00:20:07.610 align:middle line:84%
But also, if I look at intersect
complement, what is this?

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:09.080 align:middle line:90%
This is equal to--

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:14.890 align:middle line:84%
now k, E k for k
going from 1 to m,

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:19.810 align:middle line:84%
these are all contained in
the complement of E m plus 1

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:21.290 align:middle line:90%
because they're disjoint.

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:28.570 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to
just A intersect union

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:34.030 align:middle line:90%
A equals 1 to m E k.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:39.620 align:middle line:90%


00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:42.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, I got ahead of
myself a minute ago,

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:44.900 align:middle line:90%
but now we're at this stage.

00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:48.340 align:middle line:90%


00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:58.190 align:middle line:84%
Since E m plus 1 is
measurable, the measure

00:20:58.190 --> 00:21:08.570 align:middle line:84%
of A intersect this union A
equals 1 m plus 1 E k, this

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:16.220 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the outer
measure of A intersect k

00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:23.840 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 m plus 1
E k intersect E m

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:33.390 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 plus the outer measure
of A intersect union k

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:47.520 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 m plus 1 E k intersect
E m plus 1 complement.

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:50.970 align:middle line:84%
Just using that E n
plus 1 is measurable.

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:53.670 align:middle line:84%
And now, we plug in
what these things are.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:22:00.310 align:middle line:84%
So this whole thing is equal
to A intersect E m plus 1.

00:22:00.310 --> 00:22:08.290 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to the measure
of A intersect E m plus 1.

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:14.700 align:middle line:84%
And now, this whole thing
is equal to the measure

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:18.795 align:middle line:84%
of A intersect the union
only going up to m.

00:22:18.795 --> 00:22:22.640 align:middle line:90%


00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:25.280 align:middle line:84%
And now, this is where we
use the induction hypothesis

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:30.750 align:middle line:84%
because now we have a union
of m disjoint measurable sets.

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:41.630 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal
to plus sum from k

00:22:41.630 --> 00:22:50.400 align:middle line:90%
equals 1 to m A intersect E k.

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:54.870 align:middle line:84%
So this is by our
induction hypothesis.

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:57.710 align:middle line:90%


00:22:57.710 --> 00:23:01.880 align:middle line:84%
And combining these two
terms is exactly what we

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:05.150 align:middle line:84%
want for n equals
m plus 1, k equals

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:15.510 align:middle line:84%
1 m plus 1 outer measure
of A intersect E k.

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:16.590 align:middle line:90%
And that's the proof.

00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:30.080 align:middle line:90%


00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:38.730 align:middle line:84%
So now using this theorem
and the lemma before it,

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:40.695 align:middle line:84%
we will prove that
the collection

00:23:40.695 --> 00:23:43.395 align:middle line:84%
m of Lebesgue measurable
sets is a sigma algebra.

00:23:43.395 --> 00:23:48.120 align:middle line:90%


00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:50.320 align:middle line:84%
We already know it's an
algebra up to this point.

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:52.350 align:middle line:84%
But we just need to verify
it's a sigma algebra.

00:23:52.350 --> 00:24:03.510 align:middle line:90%


00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:04.560 align:middle line:90%
So what's the proof?

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:11.840 align:middle line:84%
Now, again, based
on this remark here,

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:16.535 align:middle line:84%
we only need to
verify that if I have

00:24:16.535 --> 00:24:22.970 align:middle line:84%
a countable collection of
disjoint measurable sets, then

00:24:22.970 --> 00:24:24.620 align:middle line:90%
the union is measurable.

00:24:24.620 --> 00:24:27.260 align:middle line:84%
I don't have to check
it for every collection

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:31.280 align:middle line:84%
of measurable sets, just
disjoint measurable sets.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:35.410 align:middle line:90%


00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:36.860 align:middle line:90%
So we just need to check.

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:53.080 align:middle line:84%
So we've already
checked it's an algebra.

00:24:53.080 --> 00:25:03.240 align:middle line:84%
So by the lemma, the first thing
we proved during this lecture,

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:26.140 align:middle line:84%
we just need to show m is closed
under taking countable disjoint

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:28.990 align:middle line:84%
unions, meaning if I have
a countable collection

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:30.520 align:middle line:84%
of disjoint measurable
sets, I need

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:32.540 align:middle line:90%
to show the union is measurable.

00:25:32.540 --> 00:25:46.240 align:middle line:84%
So let E sub n be a
countable collection here--

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:47.770 align:middle line:90%
n is a natural number--

00:25:47.770 --> 00:25:54.030 align:middle line:90%
of disjoint measurable sets.

00:25:54.030 --> 00:25:56.800 align:middle line:90%


00:25:56.800 --> 00:25:59.970 align:middle line:84%
So we need to verify the
definition of being measurable.

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:05.910 align:middle line:84%
But remember, that reduces
really to one inequality,

00:26:05.910 --> 00:26:11.870 align:middle line:84%
since one of those
inequalities is always clear.

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:21.960 align:middle line:84%
So let A be a subset of R, and
let's denote E to be the union.

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:25.210 align:middle line:90%


00:26:25.210 --> 00:26:26.410 align:middle line:90%
So what do we need to show?

00:26:26.410 --> 00:26:31.780 align:middle line:90%


00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:37.380 align:middle line:84%
We want to show that the
outer measure of A intersect E

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:43.200 align:middle line:84%
complement plus the outer
measure of A intersect E--

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:45.990 align:middle line:90%
E here, again, is a union--

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:48.420 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
outer measure of A.

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:51.750 align:middle line:84%
But we always have
the outer measure of A

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:54.495 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to this,
so we just need to show this.

00:26:54.495 --> 00:27:21.070 align:middle line:90%


00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:23.710 align:middle line:90%
So let's do this.

00:27:23.710 --> 00:27:28.550 align:middle line:90%


00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:29.990 align:middle line:90%
Let N be a natural number.

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:37.630 align:middle line:84%
So let N be a
natural number by--

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:51.510 align:middle line:84%
so since M is an algebra,
this, a finite union,

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:58.250 align:middle line:84%
n equals 1 to capital N
of E sub n is measurable.

00:27:58.250 --> 00:28:06.640 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if I want
the outer measure of A,

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:12.040 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to
the outer measure

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:18.940 align:middle line:84%
of A intersect n
equals 1 to N E sub

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:27.640 align:middle line:84%
n plus the outer
measure of A intersect n

00:28:27.640 --> 00:28:34.120 align:middle line:90%
equals 1 to N E n complement.

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:40.450 align:middle line:84%
Now, this finite
union is contained

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:44.750 align:middle line:90%
in E. E is the total union.

00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:49.130 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the complement
of this finite union

00:28:49.130 --> 00:28:53.190 align:middle line:90%
contains the complement of E.

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:58.620 align:middle line:84%
So this complement here
contains the complement of E,

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:02.250 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, A
intersect this is a bigger

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:04.950 align:middle line:84%
set than A intersect
the complement of E.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:09.200 align:middle line:84%
So this is bigger than or
equal to the outer measure

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:19.070 align:middle line:84%
of A intersect union n equals 1
to N E n plus the outer measure

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:22.490 align:middle line:84%
of A intersect E
complement-- again,

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.060 align:middle line:84%
because this finite union is
contained in the whole union.

00:29:26.060 --> 00:29:28.910 align:middle line:84%
So when I take complements,
that switches around

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:30.110 align:middle line:90%
what's contained in what.

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:32.540 align:middle line:84%
So then I get A
intersect E complement

00:29:32.540 --> 00:29:34.790 align:middle line:84%
is contained in A
intersect the complement

00:29:34.790 --> 00:29:35.690 align:middle line:90%
of this finite union.

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:41.293 align:middle line:90%


00:29:41.293 --> 00:29:41.960 align:middle line:90%
And that's good.

00:29:41.960 --> 00:29:43.460 align:middle line:90%
So this is now up here.

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:47.420 align:middle line:84%
We wanted this to be smaller
than the measure of A here.

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:50.010 align:middle line:90%
And now what do we have?

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:56.270 align:middle line:84%
We have the outer measure
of A with a finite union

00:29:56.270 --> 00:29:58.160 align:middle line:90%
of disjoint measurable sets.

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:07.300 align:middle line:84%
And we can write this using the
previous theorem as sum from n

00:30:07.300 --> 00:30:12.360 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to capital
N outer measure

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:16.515 align:middle line:90%
of A intersect E n plus--

00:30:16.515 --> 00:30:20.910 align:middle line:90%


00:30:20.910 --> 00:30:24.030 align:middle line:90%
just keeping the second term.

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:27.450 align:middle line:84%
Now, this holds for
every N. N was arbitrary.

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:32.380 align:middle line:90%


00:30:32.380 --> 00:30:34.480 align:middle line:84%
So I can let capital
N go to infinity.

00:30:34.480 --> 00:30:37.840 align:middle line:84%
Remember, I've shown that
the outer measure of A

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:40.790 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than or equal
to this quantity here.

00:30:40.790 --> 00:30:44.350 align:middle line:84%
So I can let N go to
infinity to conclude

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:48.610 align:middle line:84%
that the outer measure of A
is bigger than or equal to sum

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:52.090 align:middle line:84%
from n equals 1 to infinity
of the outer measure

00:30:52.090 --> 00:31:02.650 align:middle line:84%
of A intersect E n plus
the outer measure of the A

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:04.960 align:middle line:84%
intersect the
complement of the union.

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:08.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, remember what we
proved about outer measure--

00:31:08.970 --> 00:31:11.700 align:middle line:84%
that the sum of
the outer measures

00:31:11.700 --> 00:31:16.350 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than or equal to the
outer measure of the union.

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:19.860 align:middle line:84%
So this thing is bigger than
or equal to the outer measure

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:26.190 align:middle line:84%
of the union over
all N A intersect E

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:32.980 align:middle line:90%
n plus A intersect E complement.

00:31:32.980 --> 00:31:39.090 align:middle line:84%
And this is just equal
to plus the outer measure

00:31:39.090 --> 00:31:42.990 align:middle line:90%
of A intersect E complement.

00:31:42.990 --> 00:31:48.950 align:middle line:90%


00:31:48.950 --> 00:31:56.320 align:middle line:84%
So we've shown
that the collection

00:31:56.320 --> 00:31:59.170 align:middle line:84%
of all Lebesgue
measurable subsets of R

00:31:59.170 --> 00:32:00.340 align:middle line:90%
form a sigma algebra.

00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:08.230 align:middle line:90%


00:32:08.230 --> 00:32:11.170 align:middle line:90%
So let me just--

00:32:11.170 --> 00:32:15.540 align:middle line:84%
maybe I should have
said this at the start,

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:17.490 align:middle line:84%
but let me just pause
here for a second.

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:20.520 align:middle line:84%
And maybe you're wondering
why all this sigma algebra

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:23.050 align:middle line:90%
business anyways?

00:32:23.050 --> 00:32:24.570 align:middle line:84%
Why have I imposed
this condition?

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:29.475 align:middle line:84%
Am I just making this
definition up at as I go along?

00:32:29.475 --> 00:32:34.150 align:middle line:90%


00:32:34.150 --> 00:32:36.130 align:middle line:84%
But it's kind of
a condition that's

00:32:36.130 --> 00:32:39.610 align:middle line:84%
forced upon us based
on our expectations,

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:42.933 align:middle line:90%
in the following sense--

00:32:42.933 --> 00:32:44.350 align:middle line:84%
you remember one
of the properties

00:32:44.350 --> 00:32:49.030 align:middle line:84%
that we wanted of Lebesgue
measure, or measure in general,

00:32:49.030 --> 00:32:54.490 align:middle line:84%
was that the measure
of a countable union

00:32:54.490 --> 00:33:00.020 align:middle line:84%
of disjoint sets is equal
to the sum of the measures.

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:05.810 align:middle line:84%
And I stated without
proof-- but there is a proof

00:33:05.810 --> 00:33:11.180 align:middle line:84%
in the textbook-- that you
cannot have a measure defined

00:33:11.180 --> 00:33:15.410 align:middle line:84%
on every subset satisfying those
properties that we outlined.

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:18.410 align:middle line:84%
So you have to have some
collection of subsets

00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:21.650 align:middle line:84%
on which you have
these properties,

00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:24.590 align:middle line:84%
that the measure of an interval
is the length of the interval,

00:33:24.590 --> 00:33:26.900 align:middle line:84%
and that's
translation invariant,

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:28.940 align:middle line:84%
and that the
measure of the union

00:33:28.940 --> 00:33:30.680 align:middle line:90%
is the sum of the measures.

00:33:30.680 --> 00:33:35.300 align:middle line:84%
That last statement,
though, hidden in there,

00:33:35.300 --> 00:33:37.010 align:middle line:84%
there's kind of a
subtle assumption--

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:39.320 align:middle line:84%
that if you have
a measure defined

00:33:39.320 --> 00:33:43.940 align:middle line:84%
on some collection of subsets,
then that collection of subsets

00:33:43.940 --> 00:33:47.420 align:middle line:84%
better be closed under
taking countable unions.

00:33:47.420 --> 00:33:50.300 align:middle line:84%
If I'm to be able to make
the statement that I want,

00:33:50.300 --> 00:33:55.070 align:middle line:84%
that the measure of a countable
union of disjoint sets

00:33:55.070 --> 00:34:00.380 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the sum of the
measures, then hidden in there

00:34:00.380 --> 00:34:04.460 align:middle line:84%
is that my measure has to be
defined for a countable union

00:34:04.460 --> 00:34:06.350 align:middle line:90%
of measurable sets.

00:34:06.350 --> 00:34:09.500 align:middle line:84%
In other words, if I have
a countable collection

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:12.650 align:middle line:84%
of measurable sets,
then for the statement

00:34:12.650 --> 00:34:14.810 align:middle line:84%
I want to even make
sense, I should

00:34:14.810 --> 00:34:17.449 align:middle line:84%
have that the union of
this countable collection

00:34:17.449 --> 00:34:21.230 align:middle line:84%
of measurable sets is
contained in the class of sets

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:23.750 align:middle line:90%
that I'm measuring.

00:34:23.750 --> 00:34:31.250 align:middle line:84%
And so before we even
discussed outer measure,

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:34.429 align:middle line:84%
all that, how you
could maybe see

00:34:34.429 --> 00:34:39.080 align:middle line:84%
coming that we would have
some condition like the class

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:41.239 align:middle line:84%
of sets that we're going
to measure will be a sigma

00:34:41.239 --> 00:34:43.820 align:middle line:84%
algebra, or should
be a sigma algebra,

00:34:43.820 --> 00:34:48.389 align:middle line:84%
should be closed under
taking countable unions.

00:34:48.389 --> 00:34:50.897 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I was rambling,
but hopefully, you

00:34:50.897 --> 00:34:51.980 align:middle line:90%
got something out of that.

00:34:51.980 --> 00:35:03.110 align:middle line:90%


00:35:03.110 --> 00:35:18.120 align:middle line:84%
So we've shown that the
set of measurable sets

00:35:18.120 --> 00:35:19.660 align:middle line:90%
is a sigma algebra.

00:35:19.660 --> 00:35:22.920 align:middle line:84%
Now what I'm going to show
is that it contains the Borel

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:23.830 align:middle line:90%
sigma algebra.

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:26.700 align:middle line:84%
So remember, the
Borel sigma algebra

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:34.230 align:middle line:84%
is the smallest sigma algebra
that contains all open sets.

00:35:34.230 --> 00:35:39.020 align:middle line:84%
If I have any other sigma
algebra containing open sets,

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:40.610 align:middle line:84%
that contains all
open sets, then

00:35:40.610 --> 00:35:44.030 align:middle line:84%
it must contain the Borel sigma
algebra because the Borel sigma

00:35:44.030 --> 00:35:45.070 align:middle line:90%
algebra is the smallest.

00:35:45.070 --> 00:35:52.160 align:middle line:90%


00:35:52.160 --> 00:35:56.510 align:middle line:84%
And I should quantify smallest--
smallest meaning with respect

00:35:56.510 --> 00:35:57.950 align:middle line:90%
to inclusion.

00:35:57.950 --> 00:36:04.400 align:middle line:84%
If there's any other sigma
algebra that contains all

00:36:04.400 --> 00:36:06.740 align:middle line:84%
open sets, then the
Borel sigma algebra B

00:36:06.740 --> 00:36:08.210 align:middle line:84%
is contained in
that sigma algebra.

00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:13.050 align:middle line:90%


00:36:13.050 --> 00:36:18.915 align:middle line:84%
But first, let's prove
kind of a simpler case.

00:36:18.915 --> 00:36:23.737 align:middle line:90%


00:36:23.737 --> 00:36:25.570 align:middle line:84%
So let me state this,
and then I'll explain.

00:36:25.570 --> 00:36:34.130 align:middle line:84%
For all a in R, the open
interval a to infinity

00:36:34.130 --> 00:36:37.440 align:middle line:90%
is measurable.

00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:40.470 align:middle line:84%
So in the end, we want
to be able to show--

00:36:40.470 --> 00:36:42.330 align:middle line:84%
so we already know M
is a sigma algebra.

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:45.550 align:middle line:84%
If we can then show that
every open set is measurable,

00:36:45.550 --> 00:36:50.940 align:middle line:84%
then that means that M is
a sigma algebra containing

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:52.980 align:middle line:84%
all open sets, and
therefore, it must

00:36:52.980 --> 00:36:55.140 align:middle line:84%
contain the Borel sigma
algebra, which remember,

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:59.290 align:middle line:84%
is the smallest sigma algebra
containing all open sets.

00:36:59.290 --> 00:37:03.570 align:middle line:84%
So to build up to showing that
every open set is measurable,

00:37:03.570 --> 00:37:07.820 align:middle line:84%
let's start with
a very simple type

00:37:07.820 --> 00:37:15.045 align:middle line:84%
of open set, half open or
half infinite open interval.

00:37:15.045 --> 00:37:17.770 align:middle line:90%


00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:26.540 align:middle line:84%
So let A be a subset
of R. And let's

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:38.090 align:middle line:84%
write A1 to be A intersect a to
infinity, and A2 be A intersect

00:37:38.090 --> 00:37:45.065 align:middle line:84%
the complement, which is just
minus infinity to a closed.

00:37:45.065 --> 00:37:47.710 align:middle line:90%


00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:50.500 align:middle line:90%
So what do we want to show?

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:54.910 align:middle line:84%
To show that this
set is measurable,

00:37:54.910 --> 00:37:58.720 align:middle line:84%
we want to show that
the outer measure of A1

00:37:58.720 --> 00:38:02.920 align:middle line:84%
plus the outer
measure of A2 is less

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:08.850 align:middle line:84%
than or equal to the
outer measure of A

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:11.790 align:middle line:84%
because A1 is A
intersect a set, A2

00:38:11.790 --> 00:38:15.670 align:middle line:84%
is A intersect a
complement of that set.

00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:18.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, if the outer
measure of A is infinite,

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:21.520 align:middle line:90%
this holds regardless.

00:38:21.520 --> 00:38:29.820 align:middle line:84%
So if the outer measure
is infinite, done.

00:38:29.820 --> 00:38:36.400 align:middle line:84%
So suppose the outer
measure is finite.

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:42.533 align:middle line:90%


00:38:42.533 --> 00:38:43.950 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:38:43.950 --> 00:38:49.320 align:middle line:84%
going to show that this
sum on the left-hand side

00:38:49.320 --> 00:38:53.520 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to this
plus epsilon, where epsilon

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:54.660 align:middle line:90%
is arbitrary.

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:58.930 align:middle line:84%
And then we can send epsilon
to 0 to get the inequality.

00:38:58.930 --> 00:39:03.580 align:middle line:84%
And everything will reduce to
what we've done with intervals,

00:39:03.580 --> 00:39:05.800 align:middle line:90%
as you'll now see.

00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:27.000 align:middle line:84%
So let I n be a collection
of open intervals such that--

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:30.690 align:middle line:84%
remember, the outer
measure of A is the infimum

00:39:30.690 --> 00:39:34.110 align:middle line:84%
of the sum of lengths
of intervals covering

00:39:34.110 --> 00:39:41.790 align:middle line:84%
A-- so such that sum over n
length of I n is less than

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:46.365 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the outer
measure of A plus epsilon.

00:39:46.365 --> 00:39:56.550 align:middle line:90%


00:39:56.550 --> 00:40:09.312 align:middle line:84%
Define J n to be I n intersect
A infinity K n to be I

00:40:09.312 --> 00:40:12.740 align:middle line:84%
n intersect the
complement of A infinity.

00:40:12.740 --> 00:40:18.790 align:middle line:90%


00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:23.380 align:middle line:90%
Now, the union of the J n's--

00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:39.600 align:middle line:84%
well, first off, let me say then
each of these sets J n and K n

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:43.710 align:middle line:90%
are intervals.

00:40:43.710 --> 00:40:46.620 align:middle line:84%
They're the intersection
of two intervals,

00:40:46.620 --> 00:40:53.115 align:middle line:84%
one open and the other
closed, or empty.

00:40:53.115 --> 00:40:59.840 align:middle line:90%


00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:04.370 align:middle line:84%
Now, the union of
the I n's covers A.

00:41:04.370 --> 00:41:07.070 align:middle line:84%
So if I take the unions
of the J n's, this

00:41:07.070 --> 00:41:10.550 align:middle line:84%
will cover A
intersect A infinity.

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:14.000 align:middle line:90%


00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:22.690 align:middle line:84%
Then A1 is contained in
the union of the J n's.

00:41:22.690 --> 00:41:30.040 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, A2 is contained
in the union of the K n's.

00:41:30.040 --> 00:41:33.810 align:middle line:90%
And one more thing--

00:41:33.810 --> 00:41:39.110 align:middle line:84%
now, I n is an
interval, and it's

00:41:39.110 --> 00:41:47.170 align:middle line:84%
simple to check since just based
on this being a finite interval

00:41:47.170 --> 00:41:51.820 align:middle line:84%
that if I take
the length of I n,

00:41:51.820 --> 00:41:59.200 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to the length
of J n plus the length of K n.

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:04.203 align:middle line:84%
So I take each I n, and I split
it up into two subintervals.

00:42:04.203 --> 00:42:06.370 align:middle line:84%
They're not going to be
open intervals, necessarily.

00:42:06.370 --> 00:42:08.080 align:middle line:84%
This will be an
open interval, this

00:42:08.080 --> 00:42:12.220 align:middle line:84%
one not necessarily, but the
sum of the lengths of these two

00:42:12.220 --> 00:42:16.928 align:middle line:84%
intervals will add up to the
length of the interval I n.

00:42:16.928 --> 00:42:17.470 align:middle line:90%
That's clear.

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:25.322 align:middle line:90%


00:42:25.322 --> 00:42:42.430 align:middle line:84%
There's, if you'd like, J n
and K n will be including that.

00:42:42.430 --> 00:42:46.040 align:middle line:90%


00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:50.590 align:middle line:90%
And now, we're almost home free.

00:42:50.590 --> 00:42:52.030 align:middle line:84%
So A1 is contained
in this union.

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:53.590 align:middle line:90%
A2 is contained in this union.

00:42:53.590 --> 00:43:01.310 align:middle line:84%
So if I look at the measure
of A1 plus the measure of A2,

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:05.660 align:middle line:84%
the measure of A1, since
it's contained in this union,

00:43:05.660 --> 00:43:08.150 align:middle line:84%
is going to be less than or
equal to the sum of the outer

00:43:08.150 --> 00:43:14.000 align:middle line:90%
measures of J n's.

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:19.350 align:middle line:84%
And the measure of A2,
again, is contained in K,

00:43:19.350 --> 00:43:22.950 align:middle line:84%
in the union of the K n's,
so that's less than or equal

00:43:22.950 --> 00:43:25.740 align:middle line:84%
to the sum of the
measures of the K n's.

00:43:25.740 --> 00:43:30.570 align:middle line:84%
And now, bringing these two
together in one sum over n,

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:34.510 align:middle line:84%
and using the fact
that this is equal to--

00:43:34.510 --> 00:43:36.280 align:middle line:84%
so the outer measure
of an interval

00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:40.770 align:middle line:84%
is equal to its length,
which we proved last time--

00:43:40.770 --> 00:43:43.910 align:middle line:90%


00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:49.870 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to sum
length of the I n.

00:43:49.870 --> 00:43:51.880 align:middle line:84%
And remember, how did
we choose the I n?

00:43:51.880 --> 00:43:55.990 align:middle line:84%
We chose this I n so that
some of the lengths of the I n

00:43:55.990 --> 00:43:58.300 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the outer measure

00:43:58.300 --> 00:43:59.710 align:middle line:90%
of A plus epsilon.

00:43:59.710 --> 00:44:06.740 align:middle line:90%


00:44:06.740 --> 00:44:12.620 align:middle line:84%
So I should have added here,
let epsilon be positive.

00:44:12.620 --> 00:44:13.760 align:middle line:90%
Sorry about that.

00:44:13.760 --> 00:44:18.190 align:middle line:90%


00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:26.210 align:middle line:84%
So I've shown that for
arbitrary epsilon positive,

00:44:26.210 --> 00:44:29.598 align:middle line:84%
this number over here is
less than or equal to--

00:44:29.598 --> 00:44:30.640 align:middle line:90%
I still haven't finished.

00:44:30.640 --> 00:44:33.037 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, getting ahead of myself.

00:44:33.037 --> 00:44:34.745 align:middle line:84%
That's what happens
when you get excited.

00:44:34.745 --> 00:44:37.525 align:middle line:90%


00:44:37.525 --> 00:44:39.650 align:middle line:84%
And this is less than or
equal to the outer measure

00:44:39.650 --> 00:44:41.615 align:middle line:90%
of A plus epsilon.

00:44:41.615 --> 00:44:44.690 align:middle line:90%


00:44:44.690 --> 00:44:48.300 align:middle line:84%
So we've shown that for
arbitrary epsilon positive,

00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:50.990 align:middle line:84%
this number is less than
or equal to this number

00:44:50.990 --> 00:44:51.930 align:middle line:90%
plus epsilon.

00:44:51.930 --> 00:44:54.710 align:middle line:84%
So I can send
epsilon to 0 to get

00:44:54.710 --> 00:45:02.120 align:middle line:84%
that the outer measure of A1
plus the outer measure of A2

00:45:02.120 --> 00:45:05.058 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
outer measure of A, which

00:45:05.058 --> 00:45:06.725 align:middle line:84%
remember, that's what
we wanted to show.

00:45:06.725 --> 00:45:22.050 align:middle line:90%


00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:27.630 align:middle line:84%
So we've shown that these open
intervals from A to infinity

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:30.000 align:middle line:90%
are measurable.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:44.600 align:middle line:84%
It's now not a long trip to
saying that every open set is

00:45:44.600 --> 00:45:45.740 align:middle line:90%
Lebesgue measurable.

00:45:45.740 --> 00:45:48.410 align:middle line:90%


00:45:48.410 --> 00:46:07.680 align:middle line:84%
So theorem-- every open
set is Lebesgue measurable,

00:46:07.680 --> 00:46:12.120 align:middle line:84%
and thus, a Borel sigma algebra,
which is the smallest sigma

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:14.280 align:middle line:84%
algebra containing
all open sets,

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:17.085 align:middle line:84%
is contained in the sigma
algebra of measurable sets.

00:46:17.085 --> 00:46:32.190 align:middle line:90%


00:46:32.190 --> 00:46:35.340 align:middle line:84%
So we've shown these types
of intervals are open.

00:46:35.340 --> 00:46:38.790 align:middle line:84%
Let's show finite open
intervals are also--

00:46:38.790 --> 00:46:40.890 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we've shown these
types of open intervals

00:46:40.890 --> 00:46:41.850 align:middle line:90%
are measurable.

00:46:41.850 --> 00:46:49.800 align:middle line:84%
Let's now show that finite
open intervals are measurable.

00:46:49.800 --> 00:47:06.630 align:middle line:84%
So now, we have for all b in R
that minus infinity to b, this

00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:13.488 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the union n
equals 1 to infinity of what?

00:47:13.488 --> 00:47:22.700 align:middle line:84%
Of the union of
these half closed

00:47:22.700 --> 00:47:33.970 align:middle line:84%
intervals, which we can write
as the complement of these types

00:47:33.970 --> 00:47:36.400 align:middle line:90%
of open intervals.

00:47:36.400 --> 00:47:42.890 align:middle line:84%
Now, we just showed that these
types of intervals are open,

00:47:42.890 --> 00:47:45.650 align:middle line:84%
I mean, these types of open
intervals are measurable.

00:47:45.650 --> 00:47:47.930 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, their
complement is measurable

00:47:47.930 --> 00:47:52.380 align:middle line:84%
and the collection of measurable
sets is a sigma algebra.

00:47:52.380 --> 00:47:54.800 align:middle line:84%
So countable unions
are also measurable.

00:47:54.800 --> 00:48:01.420 align:middle line:84%
So we conclude that
this guy is measurable.

00:48:01.420 --> 00:48:14.130 align:middle line:90%


00:48:14.130 --> 00:48:21.875 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, remember for an
algebra, it's closed under--

00:48:21.875 --> 00:48:24.250 align:middle line:84%
for a sigma algebra, it's
closed under taking complements

00:48:24.250 --> 00:48:26.080 align:middle line:90%
and taking countable unions.

00:48:26.080 --> 00:48:29.860 align:middle line:84%
But by De Morgan's
laws, that means

00:48:29.860 --> 00:48:33.880 align:middle line:84%
it's also closed under
taking intersections.

00:48:33.880 --> 00:48:37.750 align:middle line:84%
We conclude that
for all a, b, and R,

00:48:37.750 --> 00:48:41.590 align:middle line:84%
any finite open
interval a, b, which

00:48:41.590 --> 00:48:50.930 align:middle line:84%
is equal to minus infinity
to b intersect a to infinity,

00:48:50.930 --> 00:48:53.140 align:middle line:84%
this is measurable by
what we just proved.

00:48:53.140 --> 00:48:55.810 align:middle line:84%
This is measurable by the
theorem we proved before.

00:48:55.810 --> 00:48:58.450 align:middle line:84%
And sigma algebras
are also closed

00:48:58.450 --> 00:48:59.890 align:middle line:90%
under taking countable unions.

00:48:59.890 --> 00:49:01.330 align:middle line:90%
This is just a finite union.

00:49:01.330 --> 00:49:04.660 align:middle line:90%
So this is also measurable.

00:49:04.660 --> 00:49:15.310 align:middle line:90%


00:49:15.310 --> 00:49:24.795 align:middle line:84%
Now, maybe you covered this
in 100B, maybe you didn't.

00:49:24.795 --> 00:49:26.420 align:middle line:84%
It's going to appear
on the assignment.

00:49:26.420 --> 00:49:30.560 align:middle line:84%
But I'll underline what's
going to be on the assignment.

00:49:30.560 --> 00:49:48.490 align:middle line:84%
But since every open subset
of R is a countable union of,

00:49:48.490 --> 00:49:57.710 align:middle line:84%
in fact, disjoint
open intervals--

00:49:57.710 --> 00:50:00.070 align:middle line:84%
open intervals meaning
it could be a finite one,

00:50:00.070 --> 00:50:01.820 align:middle line:84%
it could be one like
that, it could be one

00:50:01.820 --> 00:50:04.820 align:middle line:84%
like we wrote over there
where it's a to infinity--

00:50:04.820 --> 00:50:09.290 align:middle line:84%
but every open subset can be
written as a countable union

00:50:09.290 --> 00:50:11.120 align:middle line:90%
of disjoint open intervals.

00:50:11.120 --> 00:50:14.870 align:middle line:84%
And since open intervals,
we've now concluded,

00:50:14.870 --> 00:50:18.020 align:middle line:84%
are always
measurable, that means

00:50:18.020 --> 00:50:19.490 align:middle line:90%
their union is measurable.

00:50:19.490 --> 00:50:30.220 align:middle line:90%


00:50:30.220 --> 00:50:32.500 align:middle line:84%
We conclude that every
open set is measurable.

00:50:32.500 --> 00:50:50.500 align:middle line:90%


00:50:50.500 --> 00:50:57.130 align:middle line:84%
We now have a collection that
we've said are measurable sets.

00:50:57.130 --> 00:50:58.450 align:middle line:90%
They form a sigma algebra.

00:50:58.450 --> 00:51:02.060 align:middle line:84%
They contain the
Borel sigma algebra,

00:51:02.060 --> 00:51:05.170 align:middle line:90%
which contains all open sets.

00:51:05.170 --> 00:51:12.600 align:middle line:90%


00:51:12.600 --> 00:51:16.920 align:middle line:84%
So now, we define
Lebesgue measure.

00:51:16.920 --> 00:51:37.180 align:middle line:84%
If E is a measurable set,
the Lebesgue measure of E

00:51:37.180 --> 00:51:43.810 align:middle line:84%
is denoted by m of E.
And it's just given

00:51:43.810 --> 00:51:49.710 align:middle line:90%
by the outer measure of E.

00:51:49.710 --> 00:51:53.700 align:middle line:84%
So like I said when we first
started all this business,

00:51:53.700 --> 00:51:57.060 align:middle line:84%
Lebesgue measure is
simply outer measure

00:51:57.060 --> 00:52:03.630 align:middle line:84%
restricted to a collection
of well-behaved sets.

00:52:03.630 --> 00:52:06.480 align:middle line:84%
And you see that here, is that
Lebesgue measure is nothing

00:52:06.480 --> 00:52:10.620 align:middle line:84%
but outer measure restricted
to those sets which

00:52:10.620 --> 00:52:11.610 align:middle line:90%
we call measurable.

00:52:11.610 --> 00:52:14.100 align:middle line:84%
And measurable meant that
they had this property

00:52:14.100 --> 00:52:18.207 align:middle line:84%
that they kind of split
sets evenly with respect

00:52:18.207 --> 00:52:18.915 align:middle line:90%
to outer measure.

00:52:18.915 --> 00:52:25.780 align:middle line:90%


00:52:25.780 --> 00:52:36.000 align:middle line:84%
So immediately, we get
to a few simple things.

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:43.930 align:middle line:84%
Theorem-- if A and
B are measurable,

00:52:43.930 --> 00:52:50.050 align:middle line:84%
and A is contained in
B, then the measure of A

00:52:50.050 --> 00:52:53.140 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
measure of B. Why is this?

00:52:53.140 --> 00:52:56.530 align:middle line:84%
Again, because outer
measure satisfies that.

00:52:56.530 --> 00:52:58.420 align:middle line:84%
m of A is just the
outer measure of A,

00:52:58.420 --> 00:53:01.060 align:middle line:84%
and that's less than or equal
to the outer measure of B, which

00:53:01.060 --> 00:53:02.890 align:middle line:84%
is, by definition,
the measure of B.

00:53:02.890 --> 00:53:06.400 align:middle line:84%
So the point is that
Lebesgue measure

00:53:06.400 --> 00:53:08.680 align:middle line:84%
inherits many properties
from outer measure.

00:53:08.680 --> 00:53:10.520 align:middle line:90%
In particular, we have this.

00:53:10.520 --> 00:53:13.360 align:middle line:84%
And then we also
have, since we know--

00:53:13.360 --> 00:53:22.820 align:middle line:90%


00:53:22.820 --> 00:53:26.390 align:middle line:90%
let me make one more.

00:53:26.390 --> 00:53:37.240 align:middle line:90%


00:53:37.240 --> 00:53:43.370 align:middle line:90%
Every interval is measurable.

00:53:43.370 --> 00:53:44.990 align:middle line:90%
Let's state it this way.

00:53:44.990 --> 00:53:49.330 align:middle line:90%


00:53:49.330 --> 00:54:05.570 align:middle line:84%
If I is an interval, then I is
measurable and the measure of I

00:54:05.570 --> 00:54:13.580 align:middle line:84%
equals the length of I. So we've
shown all open intervals are

00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:14.130 align:middle line:90%
measurable.

00:54:14.130 --> 00:54:16.130 align:middle line:84%
That's what we did a
minute ago when we showed--

00:54:16.130 --> 00:54:20.300 align:middle line:90%


00:54:20.300 --> 00:54:23.300 align:middle line:84%
so first off, let me
not get ahead of myself.

00:54:23.300 --> 00:54:26.540 align:middle line:84%
If I've shown that every
I is measurable, then

00:54:26.540 --> 00:54:30.380 align:middle line:84%
since Lebesgue measure is just
a restriction of outer measure,

00:54:30.380 --> 00:54:32.510 align:middle line:84%
and the outer measure
of an interval

00:54:32.510 --> 00:54:35.450 align:middle line:84%
is the length of the
interval, this is immediate.

00:54:35.450 --> 00:54:42.920 align:middle line:84%
So I just need to verify that
every interval is measurable.

00:54:42.920 --> 00:54:46.910 align:middle line:84%
Now, we've shown every open
interval is measurable,

00:54:46.910 --> 00:54:53.360 align:middle line:84%
but from there, it's
not difficult to get

00:54:53.360 --> 00:54:56.070 align:middle line:84%
that every interval
is measurable.

00:54:56.070 --> 00:55:02.460 align:middle line:84%
So for example, if I take a
closed and bounded interval,

00:55:02.460 --> 00:55:04.810 align:middle line:90%
this is equal to--

00:55:04.810 --> 00:55:07.700 align:middle line:90%
what is this equal to?

00:55:07.700 --> 00:55:14.950 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to b infinity
complement intersect minus

00:55:14.950 --> 00:55:17.230 align:middle line:90%
infinity a complement.

00:55:17.230 --> 00:55:20.140 align:middle line:90%


00:55:20.140 --> 00:55:26.860 align:middle line:84%
So b infinity complement gives
me b to infinity, including b.

00:55:26.860 --> 00:55:29.170 align:middle line:84%
The complement of
minus infinity to a

00:55:29.170 --> 00:55:32.800 align:middle line:84%
is a to infinity, including
a, and their intersection

00:55:32.800 --> 00:55:33.760 align:middle line:90%
gives me a, b.

00:55:33.760 --> 00:55:36.520 align:middle line:84%
Now, open intervals
are measurable.

00:55:36.520 --> 00:55:38.540 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, the
complement is measurable.

00:55:38.540 --> 00:55:40.630 align:middle line:84%
So each of these
things is measurable,

00:55:40.630 --> 00:55:42.980 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, their
intersection is measurable.

00:55:42.980 --> 00:55:47.260 align:middle line:90%
So that's measurable.

00:55:47.260 --> 00:55:53.920 align:middle line:84%
And it's the same game if I
take away one of these guys,

00:55:53.920 --> 00:55:59.250 align:middle line:84%
except now, we would use
kind of a trick like that.

00:55:59.250 --> 00:56:02.910 align:middle line:90%
So let me do one of them.

00:56:02.910 --> 00:56:08.230 align:middle line:90%
Let's say we look at a, b.

00:56:08.230 --> 00:56:10.982 align:middle line:90%


00:56:10.982 --> 00:56:12.440 align:middle line:84%
Well, I mean, I
don't think we even

00:56:12.440 --> 00:56:13.690 align:middle line:90%
have to do anything like that.

00:56:13.690 --> 00:56:17.684 align:middle line:84%
Let's say I look at
something like this--

00:56:17.684 --> 00:56:31.620 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to b
intersect complement.

00:56:31.620 --> 00:56:35.520 align:middle line:84%
So a half-open interval
including a, not including b,

00:56:35.520 --> 00:56:39.555 align:middle line:84%
is equal to minus infinity
b intersect minus infinity

00:56:39.555 --> 00:56:40.890 align:middle line:90%
a complement.

00:56:40.890 --> 00:56:45.255 align:middle line:84%
Because then, this gives me
a to infinity, including a.

00:56:45.255 --> 00:56:47.757 align:middle line:84%
And this is measurable,
this is measurable,

00:56:47.757 --> 00:56:50.340 align:middle line:84%
the complement's measurable, and
therefore, the intersection's

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:50.840 align:middle line:90%
measurable.

00:56:50.840 --> 00:56:52.980 align:middle line:90%
So this is measurable.

00:56:52.980 --> 00:56:58.050 align:middle line:84%
And those are the only two
examples I'm going to do.

00:56:58.050 --> 00:57:01.020 align:middle line:84%
And basically, by taking
the complements of these,

00:57:01.020 --> 00:57:04.620 align:middle line:84%
you get the other
types of intervals.

00:57:04.620 --> 00:57:09.520 align:middle line:84%
So this is a good one because
this is one of the properties,

00:57:09.520 --> 00:57:14.860 align:middle line:84%
remember, that we
wanted of measure.

00:57:14.860 --> 00:57:17.380 align:middle line:84%
We at least wanted to be
able to measure intervals.

00:57:17.380 --> 00:57:20.290 align:middle line:84%
And we wanted the
measure of an interval,

00:57:20.290 --> 00:57:22.390 align:middle line:84%
or at least a Lebesgue
measure of an interval,

00:57:22.390 --> 00:57:24.715 align:middle line:84%
to be the length
of that interval.

00:57:24.715 --> 00:57:28.070 align:middle line:90%


00:57:28.070 --> 00:57:38.340 align:middle line:84%
And now, let's verify that
other condition that we wanted,

00:57:38.340 --> 00:57:45.190 align:middle line:84%
that the measure of a
countable disjoint union

00:57:45.190 --> 00:57:47.300 align:middle line:90%
is the sum of the measures.

00:57:47.300 --> 00:58:11.170 align:middle line:84%
So suppose E n is a
countable collection

00:58:11.170 --> 00:58:16.085 align:middle line:90%
of disjoint measurable sets.

00:58:16.085 --> 00:58:20.180 align:middle line:90%


00:58:20.180 --> 00:58:25.460 align:middle line:84%
Then the Lebesgue
measure of the union

00:58:25.460 --> 00:58:36.130 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the sum of the
Lebesgue measure of the sets.

00:58:36.130 --> 00:58:41.350 align:middle line:84%
Now this, we have one
of the inequalities here

00:58:41.350 --> 00:58:45.100 align:middle line:84%
or we always have this is
less than or equal to this

00:58:45.100 --> 00:58:47.980 align:middle line:84%
simply because that
follows from outer measure.

00:58:47.980 --> 00:58:51.130 align:middle line:84%
Remember, outer
measure satisfied this

00:58:51.130 --> 00:58:55.180 align:middle line:84%
with an inequality there, with
M star and M star, but not

00:58:55.180 --> 00:58:56.780 align:middle line:90%
equality.

00:58:56.780 --> 00:58:59.210 align:middle line:84%
Specializing to these
well-behaved sets

00:58:59.210 --> 00:59:01.430 align:middle line:90%
gives us equality, as we'll see.

00:59:01.430 --> 00:59:08.300 align:middle line:90%


00:59:08.300 --> 00:59:14.900 align:middle line:84%
So let me just reiterate
that we do have one--

00:59:14.900 --> 00:59:17.000 align:middle line:84%
we'll prove this by
showing one is less than

00:59:17.000 --> 00:59:18.950 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the
other and vice versa.

00:59:18.950 --> 00:59:22.700 align:middle line:84%
We always have this is
less than or equal to this

00:59:22.700 --> 00:59:23.900 align:middle line:90%
because of outer measure.

00:59:23.900 --> 00:59:27.920 align:middle line:90%


00:59:27.920 --> 00:59:37.370 align:middle line:84%
So first off, we
know that we have

00:59:37.370 --> 00:59:39.410 align:middle line:84%
this countable
union is measurable

00:59:39.410 --> 00:59:42.140 align:middle line:90%
because M is a sigma algebra.

00:59:42.140 --> 00:59:43.760 align:middle line:90%
We showed that already.

00:59:43.760 --> 00:59:50.630 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the measure of
the union is, by definition,

00:59:50.630 --> 00:59:55.050 align:middle line:84%
equal to the outer
measure of the union,

00:59:55.050 --> 01:00:01.800 align:middle line:84%
which is less than or equal to
the sum of the outer measures

01:00:01.800 --> 01:00:02.970 align:middle line:90%
of the E n's.

01:00:02.970 --> 01:00:05.040 align:middle line:84%
And remember, these
are all measurable.

01:00:05.040 --> 01:00:08.385 align:middle line:84%
So by definition, the outer
measure is the measure.

01:00:08.385 --> 01:00:11.840 align:middle line:90%


01:00:11.840 --> 01:00:14.560 align:middle line:84%
So this is what I meant by
we always have one inequality

01:00:14.560 --> 01:00:18.400 align:middle line:84%
or we already have one
side of this inequality

01:00:18.400 --> 01:00:20.840 align:middle line:90%
from outer measure.

01:00:20.840 --> 01:00:25.960 align:middle line:84%
So now we just need to show
the opposite inequality.

01:00:25.960 --> 01:00:31.560 align:middle line:90%


01:00:31.560 --> 01:00:37.755 align:middle line:84%
We now show sum n measure of
E n is less than or equal to--

01:00:37.755 --> 01:00:46.930 align:middle line:90%


01:00:46.930 --> 01:00:49.555 align:middle line:90%
so how do we show this?

01:00:49.555 --> 01:00:55.090 align:middle line:90%


01:00:55.090 --> 01:00:56.330 align:middle line:90%
Let m be a natural number.

01:00:56.330 --> 01:01:00.630 align:middle line:90%


01:01:00.630 --> 01:01:23.900 align:middle line:84%
Then what is the measure of
a finite union of these guys?

01:01:23.900 --> 01:01:28.080 align:middle line:84%
Well, I can write this
also as the measure.

01:01:28.080 --> 01:01:32.180 align:middle line:84%
So this is measurable,
and therefore, I

01:01:32.180 --> 01:01:36.140 align:middle line:84%
can write this as,
in fact, R intersect.

01:01:36.140 --> 01:01:39.750 align:middle line:84%
This is kind of a
stupid way to write it,

01:01:39.750 --> 01:01:41.270 align:middle line:84%
but I'm just doing
it this way so

01:01:41.270 --> 01:01:45.230 align:middle line:84%
that it looks like something
we've already proven.

01:01:45.230 --> 01:01:49.350 align:middle line:84%
Earlier, we proved that
for measurable sets,

01:01:49.350 --> 01:01:53.730 align:middle line:84%
disjoint measurable sets,
the outer measure of a set

01:01:53.730 --> 01:01:57.990 align:middle line:84%
intersect a finite union
of disjoint measurable sets

01:01:57.990 --> 01:02:04.260 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the sum
of these measures.

01:02:04.260 --> 01:02:10.160 align:middle line:90%


01:02:10.160 --> 01:02:13.610 align:middle line:90%
And this is equal to E sub n.

01:02:13.610 --> 01:02:15.590 align:middle line:84%
And the outer measure
of a measurable set

01:02:15.590 --> 01:02:18.050 align:middle line:84%
is, by definition again,
the measure of the set.

01:02:18.050 --> 01:02:25.590 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to n
equals 1 to E sub n.

01:02:25.590 --> 01:02:29.040 align:middle line:84%
So what we've shown is that
in fact, for a finite disjoint

01:02:29.040 --> 01:02:32.970 align:middle line:84%
union, the measure is equal
to the sum of the measures.

01:02:32.970 --> 01:02:35.595 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we already did prove
that, except with an A there.

01:02:35.595 --> 01:02:38.340 align:middle line:90%


01:02:38.340 --> 01:02:41.090 align:middle line:84%
But this gives us the
opposite inequality

01:02:41.090 --> 01:02:52.000 align:middle line:84%
once we realize that
we have this sum which

01:02:52.000 --> 01:03:01.380 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
measure of, and which

01:03:01.380 --> 01:03:04.965 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
measure of the big total union.

01:03:04.965 --> 01:03:08.320 align:middle line:90%


01:03:08.320 --> 01:03:11.950 align:middle line:84%
Because this set is
contained in this set.

01:03:11.950 --> 01:03:17.310 align:middle line:84%
So what I have is
that N was arbitrary.

01:03:17.310 --> 01:03:21.030 align:middle line:84%
I had that thing on the
left-hand side is less than

01:03:21.030 --> 01:03:26.770 align:middle line:84%
or equal to this thing on the
right-hand side for arbitrary

01:03:26.770 --> 01:03:33.730 align:middle line:84%
N. So now I let N go to
infinity to conclude that--

01:03:33.730 --> 01:03:42.750 align:middle line:90%


01:03:42.750 --> 01:03:43.455 align:middle line:90%
as desired.

01:03:43.455 --> 01:03:47.710 align:middle line:90%


01:03:47.710 --> 01:03:50.290 align:middle line:84%
So this is that other
property that we wanted,

01:03:50.290 --> 01:03:56.150 align:middle line:84%
that the measure
of a disjoint union

01:03:56.150 --> 01:03:58.550 align:middle line:90%
is the sum of the measures.

01:03:58.550 --> 01:04:00.050 align:middle line:84%
Now, there was
that last property

01:04:00.050 --> 01:04:06.410 align:middle line:84%
we wanted that measure
is translation invariant.

01:04:06.410 --> 01:04:10.595 align:middle line:84%
That will be in the assignment,
so let me state it here.

01:04:10.595 --> 01:04:13.900 align:middle line:90%


01:04:13.900 --> 01:04:17.760 align:middle line:84%
So what you'll prove
in the assignment is

01:04:17.760 --> 01:04:24.780 align:middle line:84%
if E is a measurable
set and x is in R, then

01:04:24.780 --> 01:04:31.020 align:middle line:84%
the shift of the set x E plus
x, which is the set of all y

01:04:31.020 --> 01:04:38.790 align:middle line:84%
plus x such that y is
in E, is measurable.

01:04:38.790 --> 01:04:47.110 align:middle line:84%
And measure of E equals
measure of E plus x.

01:04:47.110 --> 01:04:57.770 align:middle line:84%
So this will be in the
next assignment, which

01:04:57.770 --> 01:05:04.300 align:middle line:84%
will be assignment, I think,
4, which is the third property

01:05:04.300 --> 01:05:05.990 align:middle line:90%
that we desired.

01:05:05.990 --> 01:05:11.230 align:middle line:84%
So Lebesgue measure, which is
just outer measure restricted

01:05:11.230 --> 01:05:14.920 align:middle line:84%
to the class of measurable
subsets, which is a sigma

01:05:14.920 --> 01:05:21.180 align:middle line:84%
algebra, satisfies
the three major things

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:23.340 align:middle line:90%
we wanted of a measure.

01:05:23.340 --> 01:05:26.970 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, the measure is
not defined on all subsets,

01:05:26.970 --> 01:05:33.220 align:middle line:84%
but it's defined on
a large class of--

01:05:33.220 --> 01:05:36.220 align:middle line:84%
a very rich class of
subsets of real numbers

01:05:36.220 --> 01:05:41.980 align:middle line:84%
because it contains open sets,
closed sets, and like I said,

01:05:41.980 --> 01:05:46.263 align:middle line:84%
sigma algebras are closed under
taking countable intersections

01:05:46.263 --> 01:05:46.930 align:middle line:90%
and complements.

01:05:46.930 --> 01:05:48.910 align:middle line:84%
So you could take a
collection of open sets

01:05:48.910 --> 01:05:53.800 align:middle line:84%
and take its intersection, which
is not necessarily an open set,

01:05:53.800 --> 01:05:55.730 align:middle line:84%
but that would be in
the sigma algebra.

01:05:55.730 --> 01:05:58.150 align:middle line:84%
And then you could
take a countable union

01:05:58.150 --> 01:06:01.852 align:middle line:84%
of those types of sets and still
remain in the sigma algebra.

01:06:01.852 --> 01:06:03.310 align:middle line:84%
And then you could
take complements

01:06:03.310 --> 01:06:06.830 align:middle line:84%
of those types of sets and
stay in the sigma algebra.

01:06:06.830 --> 01:06:10.090 align:middle line:84%
So like my instructor said,
if you can write down the set,

01:06:10.090 --> 01:06:11.590 align:middle line:90%
chances are it's measurable.

01:06:11.590 --> 01:06:16.140 align:middle line:90%


01:06:16.140 --> 01:06:23.190 align:middle line:84%
So one last theorem we'll
prove about measure,

01:06:23.190 --> 01:06:28.030 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll call
it a day, and call it

01:06:28.030 --> 01:06:31.970 align:middle line:84%
for the theory of
measure by itself.

01:06:31.970 --> 01:06:36.370 align:middle line:84%
Then we'll move on to
measurable functions,

01:06:36.370 --> 01:06:40.840 align:middle line:84%
and then Lebesgue integration
of measurable functions

01:06:40.840 --> 01:06:52.800 align:middle line:84%
is the following, if you
like continuity of measure,

01:06:52.800 --> 01:06:54.120 align:middle line:90%
which is the following.

01:06:54.120 --> 01:07:12.990 align:middle line:84%
So suppose E k is a
collection of measurable sets

01:07:12.990 --> 01:07:20.540 align:middle line:84%
such that E1 is contained in E2
is contained in E3, and so on.

01:07:20.540 --> 01:07:23.990 align:middle line:90%


01:07:23.990 --> 01:07:37.250 align:middle line:84%
Then the measure of k from
1 to infinity of E k--

01:07:37.250 --> 01:07:45.650 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to limit as n goes
to infinity of the measure of k

01:07:45.650 --> 01:07:55.080 align:middle line:84%
equals n of E k, which equals
limit as n goes to infinity

01:07:55.080 --> 01:07:58.200 align:middle line:90%
of the measure of E n.

01:07:58.200 --> 01:08:01.460 align:middle line:90%


01:08:01.460 --> 01:08:08.460 align:middle line:84%
Now, I just need to show
this is equal to this.

01:08:08.460 --> 01:08:12.720 align:middle line:84%
And that's what I'll show, is
that this is equal to this.

01:08:12.720 --> 01:08:14.580 align:middle line:84%
The fact that this
is equal to this

01:08:14.580 --> 01:08:18.960 align:middle line:84%
follows from the assumption
that they're nested.

01:08:18.960 --> 01:08:21.779 align:middle line:84%
E1 is contained in E2 is
contained in up to E n,

01:08:21.779 --> 01:08:25.080 align:middle line:90%
so the union is equal to E n.

01:08:25.080 --> 01:08:30.040 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just going to show that
these two things underlined

01:08:30.040 --> 01:08:31.990 align:middle line:84%
in yellow are equal
to each other.

01:08:31.990 --> 01:08:34.720 align:middle line:84%
And then the fact that this
is equal to that just follows

01:08:34.720 --> 01:08:36.250 align:middle line:90%
from this assumption here.

01:08:36.250 --> 01:08:42.420 align:middle line:90%


01:08:42.420 --> 01:08:46.770 align:middle line:84%
So for the proof, I'm going
to do this trick again

01:08:46.770 --> 01:08:52.080 align:middle line:84%
where we're going to write the
union, this countable union,

01:08:52.080 --> 01:08:59.229 align:middle line:90%
as a union of disjoint sets.

01:08:59.229 --> 01:09:10.450 align:middle line:84%
So we let F1 equal to be E1 F
k plus 1 equal to E k plus 1

01:09:10.450 --> 01:09:15.354 align:middle line:84%
take away E k for k
bigger than or equal to 1.

01:09:15.354 --> 01:09:23.270 align:middle line:90%


01:09:23.270 --> 01:09:29.270 align:middle line:84%
And let me just remark, this is
equal to E k plus 1 intersect E

01:09:29.270 --> 01:09:31.970 align:middle line:84%
k complement, since
they're nested,

01:09:31.970 --> 01:09:36.319 align:middle line:84%
since E1 is contained in
E2 is contained, and so on.

01:09:36.319 --> 01:09:40.279 align:middle line:84%
And note that since the
E k's are measurable,

01:09:40.279 --> 01:09:42.162 align:middle line:84%
this is measurable,
this is measurable.

01:09:42.162 --> 01:09:43.370 align:middle line:90%
Its complement is measurable.

01:09:43.370 --> 01:09:44.790 align:middle line:90%
The intersection is measurable.

01:09:44.790 --> 01:09:46.939 align:middle line:90%
So each of these are measurable.

01:09:46.939 --> 01:09:50.010 align:middle line:90%


01:09:50.010 --> 01:10:06.185 align:middle line:84%
Then, F k is a disjoint
collection of measurable sets.

01:10:06.185 --> 01:10:11.500 align:middle line:90%


01:10:11.500 --> 01:10:18.610 align:middle line:84%
And for all n in N, if I look
at the union k equals 1 to n

01:10:18.610 --> 01:10:21.160 align:middle line:90%
of F k--

01:10:21.160 --> 01:10:24.100 align:middle line:90%
so how am I building these guys?

01:10:24.100 --> 01:10:25.910 align:middle line:90%
I take F1 to be E1.

01:10:25.910 --> 01:10:28.450 align:middle line:84%
F2 is going to be E2
take away whatever

01:10:28.450 --> 01:10:33.910 align:middle line:90%
was already contained in E1.

01:10:33.910 --> 01:10:37.270 align:middle line:84%
F3 is whatever E3 is take away
everything that was already

01:10:37.270 --> 01:10:38.470 align:middle line:90%
appearing in E2.

01:10:38.470 --> 01:10:44.650 align:middle line:84%
So you can check that the union
from k equals 1 to n of F k

01:10:44.650 --> 01:11:03.140 align:middle line:84%
is equal to E n, and therefore
also, that the union is

01:11:03.140 --> 01:11:07.855 align:middle line:90%
equal to this union.

01:11:07.855 --> 01:11:26.820 align:middle line:90%


01:11:26.820 --> 01:11:31.200 align:middle line:84%
And now, we conclude that
if I take the measure of k

01:11:31.200 --> 01:11:42.460 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to infinity of E k,
this is equal to this union

01:11:42.460 --> 01:11:45.830 align:middle line:84%
here, which is a union of
disjoint measurable sets.

01:11:45.830 --> 01:11:48.490 align:middle line:84%
So this is equal to
the sum of k equals 1

01:11:48.490 --> 01:11:54.070 align:middle line:84%
to infinity of the
measure of F sub k, which

01:11:54.070 --> 01:11:58.840 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the limit
as n goes to infinity

01:11:58.840 --> 01:12:03.100 align:middle line:90%
k equals 1 to n measure of F k.

01:12:03.100 --> 01:12:09.090 align:middle line:84%
And again, if you like,
I can rewrite this

01:12:09.090 --> 01:12:15.780 align:middle line:84%
as limit as n goes to infinity,
this finite sum of measures

01:12:15.780 --> 01:12:21.690 align:middle line:84%
of these disjoint sets, as
the measure of k equals 1 to n

01:12:21.690 --> 01:12:31.380 align:middle line:84%
of F k which equals,
as we noted right here,

01:12:31.380 --> 01:12:35.710 align:middle line:90%
is equal to the measure of E n.

01:12:35.710 --> 01:12:44.100 align:middle line:90%


01:12:44.100 --> 01:12:49.020 align:middle line:84%
So that takes care of the
definition of Lebesgue measure.

01:12:49.020 --> 01:12:57.450 align:middle line:84%
Next time, we will define
Lebesgue measurable functions,

01:12:57.450 --> 01:13:06.990 align:middle line:90%
which are, in a certain sense--

01:13:06.990 --> 01:13:09.480 align:middle line:84%
with respect to
integration-- the analog

01:13:09.480 --> 01:13:11.110 align:middle line:90%
of continuous functions.

01:13:11.110 --> 01:13:14.550 align:middle line:84%
So continuous functions
have the property

01:13:14.550 --> 01:13:22.510 align:middle line:84%
that if I have an open
set in the target,

01:13:22.510 --> 01:13:25.560 align:middle line:84%
so if F is a function
going from x to y,

01:13:25.560 --> 01:13:28.050 align:middle line:84%
if I have a open set in
y, then the inverse image

01:13:28.050 --> 01:13:31.230 align:middle line:84%
of that open set is
an open set in x.

01:13:31.230 --> 01:13:35.320 align:middle line:84%
Measurable functions
will be similar to that,

01:13:35.320 --> 01:13:39.360 align:middle line:84%
except now with measurable
sets, but not quite.

01:13:39.360 --> 01:13:42.630 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to ask that they
take Lebesgue measurable sets

01:13:42.630 --> 01:13:46.350 align:middle line:84%
to Lebesgue measurable sets,
but Borel measurable sets

01:13:46.350 --> 01:13:53.050 align:middle line:84%
to the inverse image should
be a Lebesgue measurable set.

01:13:53.050 --> 01:13:55.020 align:middle line:90%
We'll stop there.

01:13:55.020 --> 01:14:02.000 align:middle line:90%